    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Abandoned by his father's people as a child, Temüjin built a following through personal loyalty and alliances and by 1204 had defeated every rival on the Mongolian steppe.</li><li>As Genghis Khan from 1206 he replaced tribal loyalties with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decimal-military-organisation">decimal military system</a> and commanders chosen for merit, bound to the khan and his family.</li><li>His campaigns against the Western Xia, the Jin and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> killed millions and devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, while opening trade and exchange across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a>, the main source for his youth, is often critical of its subject: it presents Genghis Khan as indecisive and as having a phobia of dogs, and it records that he killed his own half-brother.</p></aside>
    </section>

